concept :the_catchment_list do
  name "The Catchment List"
  tags :water, :ecology, :agriculture, :governance
  prominence :recognized

  prose <<~PROSE
    What is written down before anything is planted above an intake. Every organism deliberately established in a catchment feeding a #{ref :the_himalayan_holds, "Holds"} intake is entered on that intake's list before it goes into the ground: what it is, what it needs to finish a life cycle, what would end it, and which party put it there. An intake whose list has a gap in it is an intake that has to be filtered.

    The arithmetic behind that sentence sits on the list's first page in every copy. Source water reaches the monastery beds clean enough for living filtration only while what arrives in it is known; machinery doing the same work at the same volume has to be carried above five thousand metres, powered, cooled and manned through a winter, and the estimate for one intake is entered in tonnes lifted, kilowatts and winter crew. No copy of any list disputes that figure.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :mechanics, heading: "Four Lines And A Term"
    An entry is four lines: the organism, the requirement, the ender, the party. The requirement line carries what the organism cannot complete a cycle without. The ender line carries the condition that stops it, which for most entries is a temperature held across a season length, and an entry whose ender is a cold winter has to be shown a cold winter before the entry stands. That is what the #{ref :thorong_cold_house, "Thorong cold house"} is for.

    A #{ref :programmable_ecologies, "programmed ecology"} is not one entry. It is a set of organisms depending on each other, entered as a set with the dependencies drawn between the lines, struck as a set, and pulled as a set. It is also entered in the year it is planted rather than the year it finishes arriving, so most of what stands on a list has not yet done what it was planted to do.

    A list runs a term of ten years. At five it is read through in full against the ground by whoever is at the intake: every entry walked to and found. An entry that cannot be found on the mid-term reading is struck, and a struck entry closes the term.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "Who The List Binds"
    Nobody grants a term and nobody can revoke one. Both takers keep a copy because a planting one of them did not list is upstream of them both, and an intake that has to be filtered is filtered for everyone drawing off it. The intake copy is held at the monastery that maintains the beds; the second copy is kept at the nearest instrumented glacier station and carried down on the same schedule as the ice readings.

    There is no procedure for refusing an entry. A planting found in a catchment and not on the list is struck and pulled, and the party that put it in is written on the list's cover for the remainder of the term. Two covers in the eastern Holds carry a party's name. #{ref :the_overwintered_set, "The Overwintered Set"} is the reading that put one there.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — The list is a condition on avoiding filtration rather than a permit to plant, taken from source-water regimes in which an unfiltered supply keeps its exemption only while every activity in the catchment is characterized, monitored and controlled, with a ten-year determination reviewed in full at five and revocable on a finding that protection has lapsed. That decided the sanction: the penalty for a gap is not a fine but the obligation to build and man a filter plant, which is why both takers keep the same list without any agreement between them."
end
